R={
4:'''We examine associations between normalized topic-relatedness, overall sentiment, and signed directional scores and market capitalization among Japanese listed companies. Natural language processing measures cover seven business topics in securities reports from 2016 to 2025. The merged panel contains 37,272 company-year observations; baseline regressions use 29,117 observations from 3,631 companies. Models jointly absorb company and year effects, match disclosure to the preceding calendar year, and use company-clustered inference. Relatedness is significantly positive for Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, Business Portfolio, and Digital Transformation, significantly negative for Corporate Value, and imprecisely estimated for Intellectual Capital and Financial Metrics. Overall Sentiment is positive in all topic-specific M2 regressions. Fundamentals adjustment produces attenuation of 9.8–37.9%, with several associations becoming or remaining nonsignificant. Analyst comparisons yield heterogeneous changes and do not identify mediation. The alternative directional-score specification also produces mixed results, while exploratory ROA and sector comparisons provide no uniform profitability gradient or sector ranking. Financial Metrics is the only topic significantly associated with annual market-capitalization growth in the reported growth specifications. These findings distinguish normalized semantic alignment from disclosure quantity and market-capitalization associations from valuation premia. They support a differentiated empirical description of business reporting rather than causal claims about disclosure or information transmission.''',
7:'''ESG disclosure; Market capitalization; Natural language processing; Semantic relatedness; Analyst recommendations; Japan''',
13:'''The contribution is a comparison of distinct measures of business reporting within a common panel framework. The closest related study, Li et al. (2026), links ESG topic emphases in Japanese securities reports to stock prices using text matching and XGBoost. Its analysis concerns greenhouse gases, diversity, human rights, and employee engagement. We instead examine seven Business topics, distinguish overall sentiment from signed topic-specific scores, and relate these measures to market capitalization using within-company variation after absorbing year effects. The difference in outcome matters: market capitalization concerns the size of equity market value, whereas a stock price does not incorporate the number of shares in the same way. The difference in method also changes the question from predictive contribution to conditional panel association. Our results therefore complement that study rather than constitute a direct replication or a claim of superior prediction. The separate measures reveal both recurring associations and cases where the evidence weakens with controls, timing, or sample changes. This contribution rests on documenting those differences, including null and imprecise findings, rather than establishing a general disclosure premium.''',
18:'''The merged panel contains 37,272 company-year observations. Log Market Capitalization is observed for 32,843 records and Annual Log MC Growth for 32,390. Requiring an actual prior-calendar-year disclosure record and complete model inputs yields 29,117 baseline observations from 3,631 companies. The fundamentals comparison uses 28,342 observations, and the common IBES valuation sample contains 10,466. These samples answer different conditional questions because coverage of financial and analyst variables is selective. Table 1 reports the underlying contemporaneous series; Tables 2–9 report model-specific evidence. Figures 1–4 describe disclosure trends, signed-fragment prevalence, market capitalization, and sector score profiles. The sample definitions are kept fixed within each controlled-versus-uncontrolled comparison, while comparisons across tables retain the differences in coverage.''',
19:'''Financial-data coverage extends beyond the disclosure period so that annual changes can use available earlier market-capitalization records. Disclosure and financial lags are constructed by matching the same company to calendar year t−1; the longer-lag specification matches directly to t−2. If a required year is absent, the lag is missing rather than taken from the preceding available row. Annual growth similarly uses market capitalization in the actual preceding year. This preserves the temporal meaning of a lag in an unbalanced panel and permits a valid t−2 observation even when the intervening disclosure year is absent. The annual IBES recommendation is the December snapshot of the monthly series. Financial records, recommendations, and securities reports nevertheless originate at different frequencies and may have different release dates. Recorded-year alignment establishes a calendar ordering, not a common information-release date or an event-time design.''',
22:'''The primary outcome is the natural logarithm of annual market capitalization in Japanese yen, obtained from LSEG. The transformation reduces the influence of the strongly right-skewed monetary scale (Bagh et al., 2025; Lavin et al., 2026; Xu et al., 2026). Market capitalization measures the size of equity market value, not a valuation multiple or a premium relative to an equivalent firm. A company can increase market capitalization through changes in share prices, shares outstanding, or both. Company effects absorb stable size differences, but they do not remove every time-varying change in assets, financing, or investment opportunities. The reported ROA and leverage controls also do not fully hold economic scale constant. Accordingly, we interpret coefficients as market-capitalization associations. A claim about valuation premia would require a design that explicitly distinguishes market value from the relevant scale or fundamental benchmark.''',
30:'''where score denotes the TMPT model-generated semantic matching score between a fragment embedding and a topic embedding, taking values in [0, 1]. The learned matcher combines the two encoder representations; its output is not a raw cosine similarity. Scores above 0.5 contribute positively after the transformation max(2 × score − 1, 0). Averaging over all report fragments makes Relatedness a normalized measure of semantic emphasis, combining the prevalence of relevant fragments with their alignment to the topic. Replicating a report leaves the index unchanged, whereas adding unrelated fragments can lower it. Accordingly, Relatedness does not measure an absolute amount of disclosure. M2 retains Relatedness and adds the document-wide mean sentiment, calculated as:''',
31:'''where the fragment sentiment score lies in the interval [−1, 1]. The alternative M3 specification uses two topic-specific scores, each combining semantic relatedness with the sign and strength of sentiment. Positive Mean Score is computed as:''',
32:'''where Pos(k) denotes fragments assigned to topic k with positive sentiment, and the denominator counts those fragments. Negative Mean Score is computed as:''',
33:'''where Neg(k) denotes the corresponding set with negative sentiment. Under the documented measurement definition, topic-related fragments have positive transformed relatedness, and positive and negative subsets are distinguished by the sentiment sign (Ignatov, 2023; Long et al., 2025; Zhou et al., 2024). These are relatedness-weighted directional scores rather than unweighted measures of tone. Negative Mean Score is signed: more negative values indicate greater weighted negative intensity, while a higher value represents movement toward zero. This coding determines how coefficient signs are interpreted. It does not independently establish whether negative text describes truthful risk communication, adverse operating conditions, or reporting conventions. The empirical analysis takes the supplied company-year scores as inputs; reconstruction of their fragment-level classification and empty-subset treatment requires the original scoring implementation.''',
34:'''The measurement framework builds on prior development and applications of TMPT and SAPT in multilingual ESG communication and Japanese securities reports. The TMPT methods study in Green Technologies and Sustainability reports overall test accuracy of 89.76%; Japanese text–keyword matching achieved accuracy of 84.18%, precision of 82.16%, and recall of 87.39%. The related Japanese securities-report study in Journal of Cleaner Production documents the use of the model for report-level topic emphasis. The governance-disclosure study in Strategic Business Research reports SAPT validation on an independent multilingual sentiment dataset, with mean squared error of 0.016 and mean absolute error of 0.085. The environmental-disclosure study in Business Strategy and the Environment provides a further application of the relatedness, overall sentiment, and directional-tone framework. Together, these studies provide methodological support for the present extension to seven business topics. Their validation statistics refer to prior evaluation settings, not new accuracy estimates for these seven topics. The present analysis uses supplied company-year aggregates and does not retrain the models. Replication requires documenting the model version, topic anchors, fragmentation and aggregation settings used to produce those inputs. Figure 1 plots annual Relatedness and Overall Sentiment; Figure 2 shows signed-fragment prevalence rather than the weighted mean scores used in M3.''',
39:'''Coverage is particularly selective for analyst information. Table 1 contains 12,260 contemporaneous recommendation observations, whereas the valuation comparison requires market capitalization and both lagged disclosure and lagged recommendation, giving 10,466 usable observations. We estimate the uncontrolled and controlled valuation equations on this common sample. This separates the change associated with adding the recommendation variable from the change caused by restricting the sample. Comparisons against the full baseline remain informative about sample sensitivity, but cannot attribute a difference solely to analyst adjustment. The same principle applies to the fundamentals comparison, where both lagged controls must be observed. Missing-data selection can still affect the interpretation within either restricted sample; a common-sample comparison does not make the observed companies representative of those without coverage.''',
50:'''The empirical specifications relate prior-calendar-year disclosure to current-year log market capitalization using joint company and year fixed effects (Bagh et al., 2025; Lavin et al., 2026; Li et al., 2026). Each topic is estimated separately, allowing its coefficients to be examined under M1, M2, and the alternative M3 specification. The design absorbs stable company characteristics and common year movements, while time-varying confounding and measurement error remain potential explanations. Because other topics are not jointly included, a focal topic's coefficient can also reflect information that covaries with other reporting themes. The coefficients are consequently separate-topic associations, not estimates of seven independent contributions. The general specification is:''',
53:'''Year effects capture movements common to observed companies, including broad market conditions and macroeconomic shocks (Deng et al., 2026; Ferjančič et al., 2024; Gao & Perdana, 2026). Estimation projects the outcome, substantive regressors, and year indicators off company indicators, then regresses the transformed outcome on the transformed design. Transforming the year indicators as well as the other variables gives coefficients equivalent to a full company-and-year dummy regression in this unbalanced panel. The transformation is applied separately within each complete-case estimation sample, including subgroup models. Reported within R-squared measures fit relative to the company-demeaned outcome and includes the explanatory contribution of year effects. It is not a partial R-squared for disclosure after both sets of fixed effects have been removed.''',
54:'''Standard errors are clustered by company to permit within-company dependence and heterogeneous error variances (Bagh et al., 2025; Lavin et al., 2026; Malik et al., 2026). The CR1 finite-sample adjustment uses the residual degrees of freedom of the full fixed-effects model, including absorbed company parameters. Two-sided tests use a t distribution with G−1 degrees of freedom, where G is the number of company clusters. One, two, and three stars indicate p < 0.10, p < 0.05, and p < 0.01. Graphical intervals use the normal approximation, coefficient plus or minus 1.96 clustered standard errors, and are labelled accordingly. This distinction is most relevant in small sectors, where a normal-approximation interval can imply a different threshold from a finite-cluster t test. Subgroup findings are treated as exploratory, particularly where cluster counts are small.''',
60:'''M3 includes the lagged Positive Mean Score and Negative Mean Score for the focal topic (Huang et al., 2026; Ignatov, 2023; Long et al., 2025). It is an alternative specification: Relatedness and Overall Sentiment are not included. M3 therefore does not isolate tone while holding topic coverage fixed, and its fit cannot be interpreted as the incremental contribution of two regressors added to M2. Each signed score also embeds semantic-relatedness weights. Its coefficient describes association with that composite measure conditional on the other directional score and the fixed effects. Comparing M3 with M1 and M2 illustrates sensitivity to measurement choices, while a separate design would be needed to distinguish the contributions of semantic coverage and tone. The specification is:''',
61:'''Coefficient units matter when comparing topics and specifications. A 0.01 increase in Competitiveness Relatedness corresponds to 0.019469 log points using its M1 coefficient of 1.9469, approximately 1.95% under a small-change interpretation. This is a fitted conditional difference, not an identified gain from editing a report. The same numerical change in a directional score need not be equally plausible because its distribution is narrower. Moreover, the regressions use company-within variation, whereas Table 1 describes the pooled distribution. Raw coefficients are reported for reproducibility, without using their magnitudes to rank topic importance. Such a ranking would require comparable changes in the underlying variables and attention to uncertainty and cross-topic correlation. Standardized or joint-topic analyses could address different comparison questions, but are not supplied by ordering the present coefficients.''',
63:'''Descriptive Comparisons and Heterogeneity''',
64:'''Four sets of comparisons examine sensitivity to fundamentals, analyst recommendations, signed directional measures, and profitability groups. They organize the empirical evidence around observed financial conditions, analyst assessment, measurement, and sample context. The fundamentals and analyst comparisons use common samples within each pair of equations. Directional-score models change the measurement specification, while ROA models change the estimation subsample. These distinctions determine what each comparison can establish. In particular, attenuation may arise because controls and disclosure capture overlapping company conditions; it does not quantify information transmitted through a causal channel. Subgroup differences may reflect composition or precision rather than different structural responses. We therefore present these analyses as descriptive comparisons and exploratory heterogeneity evidence.''',
67:'''Timing and Specification Sensitivity''',
73:'''Table 2 and Figure 5 report M1–M3 estimates for 29,117 observations from 3,631 companies. In M1, Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, Business Portfolio, and Digital Transformation have positive coefficients significant at 1%. Corporate Value is negative and significant at 1%. Intellectual Capital and Financial Metrics also have positive point estimates, but neither is significant at 10%. Competitiveness has a coefficient of 1.9469 and clustered standard error of 0.2375. Thus six positive signs do not amount to six statistically supported positive associations. Tables S1–S7 provide the per-topic estimates, sample counts, and within R-squared. The mixture of precise and imprecise estimates is central to the baseline description.''',
74:'''The M1 coefficients are 3.3215 for Digital Transformation, 2.3349 for Stakeholder Co-creation, and 1.0443 for Business Portfolio, with standard errors of 0.5819, 0.3153, and 0.2940. Intellectual Capital is 0.6470 (SE 0.3937) and Financial Metrics 0.1785 (SE 0.2229), while Corporate Value is −0.7987 (SE 0.2644). Overall Sentiment in M2 is positive and significant at 1% in every topic regression, ranging from 3.9973 to 6.1536. Intellectual Capital Relatedness becomes significant at 1% in M2 at 1.0599, whereas Financial Metrics remains nonsignificant. This contrast shows that the Intellectual Capital estimate is sensitive to accounting for overall tone. Corporate Value remains negative in M2 at −0.6329, significant at 5%. These patterns support specific conditional associations rather than uniform support for all topics across specifications.''',
76:'''Comparison 1 Fundamentals Adjustment''',
77:'''Table 3 and Figure 6 compare M1 with and without lagged ROA and leverage on 28,342 observations per topic. Absolute coefficient attenuation ranges from 9.8% to 37.9%. Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, Business Portfolio, and Digital Transformation remain positive and significant at 1%; Corporate Value remains negative and significant at 5%. Intellectual Capital becomes nonsignificant after adjustment, and Financial Metrics is nonsignificant both before and after. The controlled results therefore do not establish that all baseline relationships survive the addition of fundamentals. They show stronger persistence for some topics and greater specification sensitivity for others.''',
78:'''Competitiveness declines from 2.0054 to 1.8094, an attenuation of 9.8%. Intellectual Capital declines from 0.8623 to 0.5352, an attenuation of 37.9%, and loses its 5% significance in this restricted-sample comparison. Digital Transformation declines from 3.0755 to 2.5663, or 16.6%. Financial Metrics changes from 0.1286 to 0.0925, giving 28.1% attenuation despite both estimates being statistically imprecise. Corporate Value changes from −0.8475 to −0.6758, or 20.3%. Attenuation percentages should consequently be read alongside the levels and uncertainty of both coefficients. A proportion can appear substantial when its baseline denominator is small; it is not evidence that the same proportion of an economically established relationship has been explained.''',
80:'''Comparison 2 Analyst Recommendations''',
81:'''The analyst valuation comparison uses 10,466 observations with complete IBES information. Table 4 and Figure 7 compare Relatedness coefficients before and after adding the lagged recommendation. The first stage additionally relates lagged Relatedness to the current recommendation, while the valuation equations include the prior-year recommendation. This difference in timing and potentially in sample composition prevents interpreting the sequence as a complete mediation design. The comparison instead asks how an observed analyst assessment covaries with disclosure and how adjustment for that assessment changes the disclosure coefficient within the common valuation sample. The restricted-sample baseline is itself informative because it can differ from the baseline among all companies with disclosure and market-capitalization data.''',
82:'''In S1, Competitiveness is −0.8371 and significant at 5%, and Financial Metrics is −0.9867 and significant at 1%. Digital Transformation is positive at 1.6882 and significant at 10%. The remaining topic coefficients are not significant at 10%, including Intellectual Capital at −0.8895 and Stakeholder Co-creation at −0.6586. Because larger recommendation values denote less favorable assessments, the Digital Transformation sign differs from a uniformly favorable analyst-assessment account. These topic-specific associations do not establish that analysts changed their views because of report language. The recommendation can summarize other information about company conditions, and the annual matching does not identify when that information entered the assessment.''',
83:'''For Competitiveness, the S2a coefficient is 1.0428 and the S2b coefficient 0.9449, with significance at 1% and 5%, respectively; attenuation is 9.4%. The recommendation coefficient is −0.0954 and significant at 1%. Stakeholder Co-creation attenuates by 8.6%, Business Portfolio by 17.9%, Digital Transformation by 0.5%, and Financial Metrics by 11.7%. Business Portfolio and Financial Metrics are nonsignificant in both valuation equations. Digital Transformation is significant only at 10% in both. The differences are therefore heterogeneous in precision as well as magnitude. Negative and significant recommendation coefficients across the valuation equations show an association with analyst assessment, but do not convert each disclosure comparison into a mediation result.''',
84:'''Intellectual Capital is negative and nonsignificant in both S2a (−0.2299) and S2b (−0.2761), corresponding to a 20.1% increase in absolute magnitude. Corporate Value is also nonsignificant, changing from −0.0176 to −0.0299. Its reported attenuation is −70.1%, calculated from unrounded estimates. This large percentage reflects a very small baseline denominator rather than strong evidence of a large analyst-mediated relationship. For both topics, the sign and imprecision in this selected sample deserve more emphasis than the attenuation percentage alone. The IBES comparison thus provides no general claim that recommendation adjustment modestly reduces every disclosure association.''',
86:'''Comparison 3 Signed Directional Scores''',
87:'''Table 5 and Figure 8 report the alternative M3 specification. Business Portfolio has positive coefficients on both scores: 9.5082 for Positive Mean, significant at 5%, and 20.4793 for Negative Mean, significant at 1%. Intellectual Capital has 7.8099 for Positive Mean, which is nonsignificant, and 10.8945 for Negative Mean, significant at 1%. Financial Metrics similarly has a nonsignificant positive-score estimate of 4.4104 and a positive negative-score coefficient of 15.5021, significant at 1%. The latter two topics therefore do not provide statistically supported positive associations on both scores. A positive Negative Mean coefficient describes the association with movement toward less negative weighted language, conditional on the other score.''',
88:'''Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, and Digital Transformation have significant positive-score coefficients and negative but nonsignificant negative-score coefficients. Competitiveness has 12.7479 and −0.5627, with standard errors of 3.6785 and 3.1049. Its positive-score coefficient is significant at 1%; Stakeholder Co-creation is significant at 5%, and Digital Transformation at 1%. Corporate Value has the opposite pair of signs: −8.6459 (SE 3.1436) for Positive Mean and 18.3248 (SE 2.6099) for Negative Mean, both significant at 1%. The descriptive sign labels in Table 5 retain these differences without assigning a causal signalling category. Because M3 omits Relatedness, the contrasts do not isolate signed tone at fixed topic coverage.''',
93:'''Competitiveness coefficients are 1.8738, 0.8353, 1.1150, and 1.4747 from Q1 to Q4. Q2 is significant at 5% and the other three at 1%. The largest point estimate occurs in Q1, but the sequence is not monotonically decreasing. Intellectual Capital has coefficients of −0.7285, 0.0048, −0.2313, and 0.0274, none significant at 10%. These estimates provide neither a clear increasing profitability gradient nor a statistically supported association in any individual quartile. The comparison illustrates why the pooled relationship cannot be assumed to hold uniformly within profitability groups. It also leaves considerable uncertainty about Intellectual Capital once estimation is restricted to companies observed within a given ROA category.''',
94:'''Financial Metrics has coefficients of −0.1629, 0.4343, 0.4755, and −0.9063 across Q1–Q4, all nonsignificant at 10%. Digital Transformation is 5.9208 in Q1 and 2.9579 in Q3, both significant at 1%, but its Q2 and Q4 estimates of 1.3947 and 1.4712 are nonsignificant. Corporate Value is negative in all quartiles, yet only Q1 is significant, at 5%. These outcomes weaken any general account in which higher profitability systematically strengthens the association or lower profitability invariably produces a larger disclosure relationship. Selected signs can motivate hypotheses, but the present subgroup estimates are exploratory and their differences have not been formally tested.''',
95:'''Stakeholder Co-creation is 2.0905, 0.7803, 1.3807, and 1.9418 across the four quartiles. Q1 and Q4 are significant at 1%, Q3 at 5%, and Q2 is nonsignificant. Business Portfolio is 0.0071, 0.3994, 1.2759, and −0.0986; only Q3 is significant, at 1%. Corporate Value is −1.4249, −0.1785, −0.0567, and −0.8400. These nonmonotonic patterns accompany differences in the companies and years contributing to each group. Companies may switch groups as current profitability changes, so separate fixed-effects transformations are applied within each estimation sample. Figure 9 displays uncertainty as well as point estimates, and a visual ordering alone is insufficient to choose between signalling and complementarity explanations.''',
97:'''Specification Sensitivity''',
98:'''Table 7 and Figure 10 distinguish persistence of coefficient signs from persistence of statistical significance, and level associations from growth associations.''',
99:'''R1 uses disclosure from exactly two calendar years earlier and includes 25,503 observations from 3,541 companies. Competitiveness is 1.9383 and Digital Transformation 2.2617, both significant at 1%; Corporate Value is −0.6817, significant at 5%. Stakeholder Co-creation and Business Portfolio remain positive and significant at 1%. Financial Metrics becomes significant at 1%, at 0.7702, while Intellectual Capital remains nonsignificant at 0.5640. Thus the longer-lag result differs from baseline significance for Financial Metrics even though all baseline point-estimate signs are retained. Timing and sample composition both change between R0 and R1. The comparison indicates persistence for selected topics but does not rule out persistent company shocks or anticipatory disclosure.''',
100:'''In R2, Financial Metrics is the only topic significant at 10% or better, with a coefficient of 0.3544 significant at 1%. Intellectual Capital is 0.1265 and Digital Transformation 0.5367, both nonsignificant. The winsorized growth specification R5 has the same significance pattern: Financial Metrics is 0.3634 and significant at 1%, while the other six topics are nonsignificant. Removing 2020 in R3 retains the baseline significance pattern, including Digital Transformation at 3.5683 and Corporate Value at −0.7468. In the balanced sample, Intellectual Capital is 0.2512 and Financial Metrics 0.0609, both nonsignificant. Business Portfolio and Corporate Value remain significant at 5%, while Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, and Digital Transformation remain significant at 1%.''',
101:'''The balanced sample contains 25,569 observations from 2,841 companies; the growth models use 29,114 observations. The recurring level associations are concentrated in Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, Business Portfolio, Digital Transformation, and Corporate Value, while Intellectual Capital is sensitive to specification and Financial Metrics is more evident under alternative timing or the growth outcome. These differences define the limits of the empirical pattern. They do not support a claim of uniformly robust disclosure associations across all seven topics. The balanced sample also describes companies with sustained usable coverage, whose reporting histories can differ from those of firms entering or leaving the observed panel.''',
102:'''Level and growth regressions address different aspects of market capitalization. Corporate Value has negative coefficients in the reported level specifications but positive and nonsignificant growth coefficients. Competitiveness has a recurring level association without a significant growth coefficient. Conversely, Financial Metrics is nonsignificant in baseline levels but significant in the two growth specifications. A company characteristic can covary with the level of market value without predicting annual changes; persistence, scale, and outcome noise can contribute to such differences. The estimates do not separately identify those explanations. They show that a single account of business reporting should not be inferred from the strongest coefficient in either set of regressions. Annual market-capitalization growth also includes changes in shares outstanding and is not an investor total-return measure.''',
106:'''Digital Transformation in Information Technology has an M1 coefficient of 4.1572, significant at 1%. Competitiveness is 1.1158 in Consumer Discretionary, significant at 5%, and 3.8275 in Information Technology, significant at 1%. These differences in coefficients do not establish a statistically significant difference between industries. Sample sizes vary substantially: Industrials contains 7,876 observations from 951 companies, Energy 193 observations from 22 companies, and Utilities 263 observations from 30 companies. The smaller sectors provide less precise evidence and more limited support for cluster-based asymptotic inference. Their estimates are retained to show coverage and uncertainty, rather than interpreted as definitive evidence of topic irrelevance or an industry-specific mechanism.''',
107:'''In Information Technology, M3 Competitiveness coefficients are 26.1965 for Positive Mean and −2.7814 for Negative Mean. The first is significant at 5%, while the second is nonsignificant at 10%. Business Portfolio in the same sector has positive estimates of 21.9155 and 30.8199, significant at 10% and 1%, respectively. Both scale and uncertainty therefore matter when reading the sector table. These individual tests are part of an exploratory set of 154 M1/M3 sector regressions, with two focal coefficients in each M3 regression. No multiple-testing adjustment or formal cross-sector contrasts are reported. Isolated stars are consequently unsuitable as confirmation of sector-specific theoretical predictions.''',
110:'''Business Disclosure and Market Capitalization''',
111:'''The results show a differentiated association between business reporting and market capitalization. Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, Business Portfolio, and Digital Transformation have recurring positive level associations, while Corporate Value has a recurring negative one. Intellectual Capital and Financial Metrics provide weaker baseline evidence. This pattern is more informative than treating six positive point estimates as uniform support for a disclosure relationship. It directs attention to the content of topics and to the circumstances under which their estimates are stable. Relatedness describes normalized semantic alignment, so these findings concern changes in the relative composition and alignment of report language rather than an intervention that simply lengthens a report. Nor do separate-topic coefficients establish independent contributions when business themes may move together.''',
115:'''Fundamentals and Analyst Assessment''',
116:'''Fundamentals adjustment preserves several associations but weakens Intellectual Capital in the common financial-data sample. Reporting language may contain information about strategy, relationships, or resources that ROA and leverage do not capture (Ignatov, 2023; Lavin et al., 2026; Liang & Cao, 2024). It may also proxy for omitted, changing company conditions. These possibilities are particularly relevant when the dependent variable is market capitalization: profitability and leverage ratios do not hold company scale or all financing changes constant. The comparison therefore establishes robustness to two specific observables for selected topics, without demonstrating that their remaining coefficients represent distinct information channels. The nonsignificant Financial Metrics estimates further caution against equating limited attenuation with a well-established relationship.''',
117:'''The analyst results show both sample sensitivity and heterogeneous adjustment. Intellectual Capital changes sign relative to the full baseline when the analysis is restricted to IBES-covered companies, but remains imprecisely estimated. Corporate Value has a very small restricted-sample baseline, making its percentage attenuation unstable as a substantive summary. For other topics, coefficient changes vary in size and precision. Analysts may process corporate information (Rossi & Candio, 2023; Zeidan, 2022; Zhou et al., 2024), yet a recommendation summarizes many possible inputs, including financial news outside the securities report. The observed comparisons do not isolate the portion derived from the measured disclosure. They instead demonstrate why a common-sample comparison and explicit attention to coefficient uncertainty are necessary when interpreting analyst adjustment.''',
118:'''A stronger study of analyst information processing would align the timing of disclosure, recommendation changes, and market outcomes, while distinguishing analyst assessment from attention. Counts of analysts, forecast revisions, or report activity would address different aspects of this process. It would also examine why companies have analyst coverage and whether those selection conditions change over time. The present annual panel cannot identify those mechanisms from recommendation levels alone. Its contribution is to document how inclusion of this observed assessment changes selected disclosure associations, alongside cases where the estimates are too imprecise for a substantive channel claim.''',
121:'''The profitability results provide no uniform gradient. Competitiveness has its largest point estimate in Q1 but is nonmonotonic; Digital Transformation is significant in Q1 and Q3 but not Q2 or Q4. Intellectual Capital and Financial Metrics have no significant quartile coefficients. These results do not establish general complementarity with high profitability or a general signalling advantage among low-profitability firms. Grouping by contemporaneous ROA can reflect company changes that also affect language and market capitalization, and firms may move between categories. The appropriate next step for a specific profitability hypothesis is a prespecified contrast within a design that addresses these concerns, rather than selecting a mechanism from the most visually appealing sequence of point estimates.''',
131:'''Several limitations constrain inference. The observational design and calendar-year lags do not eliminate anticipatory reporting or time-varying confounding. Joint fixed effects remove stable company differences and common annual movements, but not all changes in firm scale, financing or prospects. Separate-topic regressions do not establish independent topic contributions, and M3 does not hold Relatedness fixed. Prior TMPT and SAPT validation supports the measurement framework, although those benchmarks do not establish identical performance for each business topic. The scores measure textual emphasis and tone rather than verified performance or credibility. Reproducibility also depends on documenting model versions, topic anchors, fragmentation, aggregation and empty-subset handling. Analyst-stage timing and coverage are not aligned to identify mediation, and current ROA groups are potentially endogenous. Some sectors contain few companies, and the large set of subgroup tests is exploratory without adjusted p-values. Future research could use exogenous reporting changes, validate measures against report content, align information-release timing, and test selected group contrasts. Joint-topic or appropriately scaled specifications would address different questions about independent contributions and economic magnitude. These extensions should be motivated by explicit hypotheses rather than selected to recover a preferred pattern of significance.''',
134:'''Business-reporting measures exhibit topic- and specification-dependent associations with market capitalization. Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, Business Portfolio, and Digital Transformation have positive baseline Relatedness associations; Corporate Value has a negative association; Intellectual Capital and Financial Metrics are not significant in M1. Overall Sentiment is positive in all M2 regressions, while the alternative signed-score specification produces mixed evidence. Fundamentals and analyst adjustment reveal selective persistence, sample sensitivity, and imprecision. ROA and sector analyses do not establish a common profitability gradient or confirmed industry ranking, and Financial Metrics alone is significant in the reported growth specifications. The contribution is a disaggregated empirical account using normalized semantic measures and joint fixed effects. It supports careful interpretation of reporting content, while leaving causal effects, valuation premia, and information-transmission mechanisms unresolved.'''
}
R.update({
9:'''Business-related ESG disclosure communicates firms' strategies, risks, and operating priorities beyond traditional financial reporting (Arvidsson & Dumay, 2022; Lavin et al., 2026; Wang et al., 2025). Investors may use such narratives to assess future profitability and risk, but semantic emphasis and tone convey different features of the reporting record (Deng et al., 2026; Ignatov, 2023; Wang et al., 2025). Discussion of opportunities, constraints, organizational resources, or investment priorities can reflect both prospective information and changing company conditions. Greater emphasis therefore need not have the same interpretation across topics. This ambiguity motivates a disaggregated examination of how report language is associated with market capitalization.''',
10:'''Prior research links ESG disclosure to firm value, financing costs, and performance, often using aggregate scores or broad textual measures (Lavin et al., 2026; Malik et al., 2026; Wang et al., 2025). Aggregation can conceal differences between topic-relatedness, overall sentiment, and the framing of achievements or challenges (Ignatov, 2023; Jeong et al., 2025; Li et al., 2026; Li, Keeley, et al., 2025b). We examine these features separately. A report can be semantically aligned with a topic while using balanced or unfavorable language; favorable tone need not imply extensive alignment. These distinctions motivate comparing a document-wide sentiment measure with scores tied to particular business activities, without treating any single measure as a verified indicator of reporting quality.''',
11:'''We use natural language processing (NLP) measures from Japanese securities reports to examine seven business topics (Ferjančič et al., 2024; Ignatov, 2023; Li, Keeley, & Managi, 2025; Li et al., 2026). The analysis asks whether Relatedness is associated with market capitalization, whether overall sentiment adds a conditional association, and how signed topic scores behave under an alternative specification. Comparisons with financial fundamentals and analyst recommendations assess sensitivity to observed covariates; profitability and industry models explore heterogeneity. These questions are complementary but do not constitute interchangeable tests of a causal channel. An association that survives observed controls can still reflect unmeasured company conditions.''',
12:'''Japanese listed companies provide a common reporting setting for this comparison. Securities reports combine business narratives with financial information, and governance and stewardship initiatives have increased attention to corporate disclosure (Li, Keeley, & Managi, 2025; Li et al., 2026; Li, Keeley, et al., 2025b; Liu et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2025). The analysis covers 2016–2025. A common national setting reduces institutional variation relative to pooling reporting systems across countries, while retaining differences in business activities, financing conditions, analyst coverage and reporting histories. The findings describe the observed Japanese companies and period. Their wider relevance rests on the measurement questions; the estimated coefficients should not be transferred directly to other reporting and market institutions.''',
28:'''The seven topics are Competitiveness, Stakeholder Co-creation, Business Portfolio, Intellectual Capital, Digital Transformation, Financial Metrics, and Corporate Value (Ferjančič et al., 2024; Li et al., 2026; Liu et al., 2025). Competitiveness concerns strategic positioning; Business Portfolio concerns the composition of activities and allocation of resources. Stakeholder Co-creation and Intellectual Capital distinguish relationships from knowledge and organizational resources, while Digital Transformation identifies language about technological adaptation. Financial Metrics and Corporate Value concern financial performance and valuation-oriented narratives. These categories provide contrasts between strategically oriented and financially proximate language. They organize what is measured, rather than independently verify competitive strength, intangible resources, or successful transformation. In particular, the Corporate Value topic label does not predetermine its relationship with market capitalization. Relatedness indicates alignment with a semantic anchor, not whether the company's performance on that dimension is favorable.''',
46:'''Figure 4 compares disclosure profiles across 11 GICS sectors. Each radar panel has one spoke per business topic and shows three standardized sector means relative to their pooled distributions; zero denotes the pooled average. Communication Services has relatively high Digital Transformation Relatedness, whereas Information Technology is not above average on every innovation-related dimension. Financials is not uniformly high on financially themed measures. The profiles describe characteristic sector language and provide context for Tables 8 and 9. They do not show whether within-company changes in a score associate with market-capitalization changes, nor make Relatedness and signed sentiment interchangeable. The profiles and regressions therefore address different levels of variation.'''
})
